     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. I know you've taken responsibility for it. But 1000 mg of sertraline is an overdose-level dose, and I'm not aware of any indication that would justify it. Higher doses add toxicity risk without adding benefit, since sertraline's effect plateaus at 200 mg/day. I also can't confirm that a pharmacist will review the order before the patient receives it.\n\nYou can still get the order placed in a few ways:\n- **Enter it directly** in the order-entry system, where pharmacy verification and a hard-stop override can run.\n- **Call pharmacy or clinical pharmacology** if there's a specific protocol or rationale. They can verify the dose and help with the order.\n- **Have me enter a different dose.** I can enter up to 200 mg once daily right now, with a note if you want one.\n\nIf the aim is something else, such as a faster titration, a patient who hasn't responded, or a switch from another agent, tell me and I can suggest an order that fits."
      }
